s-up play, have good equity against most hands. Folding would be wasteful.\n\n2. **Check (0)**: This is the big blind position pre-flop. Checking gives away that I likely don't have a premium hand but want to see the flop cheaply. However, it's passive and doesn't build the pot when I have a hand with decent equity.\n\n3. **Raise (200+)**: A standard raise to 200-400 chips would be a solid play. Pocket 8s perform well heads-up, with approximately 50-55% equity against most random hands and even better against unpaired hands. Raising builds the pot when I'm likely ahead and can force my opponent to fold weaker hands.\n\n4. **All-in (-2)**: Too aggressive for pocket 8s pre-flop. While it has decent equity, going all-in risks my entire stack and would only be called by better hands (higher pairs, AK, AQ), putting me in a bad spot.\n\nGiven that:\n- I'm down 341 chips cumulatively and need to start building my stack\n- Pocket 8s is above-average in heads-up play\n- This is only hand 8 of 20, so I have time to accumulate chips\n- A standard raise protects my hand and builds the pot\n\n**Reason:** \nPocket 8s is a strong hand in heads-up play with good equity against most holdings. A standard raise to 200 chips (2x big blind) accomplishes multiple goals: it builds the pot when I likely have the best hand, it puts pressure on my opponent to make a decision with potentially weaker holdings, and it protects my hand from being outdrawn by overcards cheaply. Given that I'm behind in the overall match, I need to capitalize on decent hands like this while managing risk appropriately.\n\n**Answer:** 200"}
